---
deadlock_detection: false
---

# stream_preview_supersede: the live reply preview of an open thread in
# the web UI (go/tests/model/README.md) - the streamed thinking and text
# the page draws before the entry that holds them is recorded, and how
# each run of it leaves the screen again.
#
# Engine (go/internal/unreal): the projector turns a request's streamed
# fragments into live thinking-delta / assistant-delta events and, when
# the response lands, records one "thinking" entry per reasoning item
# WITH a summary and one "assistant" entry per message (project.go
# response). A retry or a newer request after shown text first sends a
# live delta-reset (project.go Delta). A steer emits a live "steer" and
# appends its input entry at once (actor.go steer); the request in
# flight keeps streaming (steer_interrupts off). Stop cuts the request:
# the gate keeps the attempt's TEXT as one assistant entry marked
# partial and drops its thinking (gate.go:241), then "cancelled" and
# "done" are recorded.
#
# Server (go/internal/serve): deltas are buffered 50ms and flushed before
# any recorded event (deltas.go), so the page sees fragments and records
# in the order the engine made them. delta-reset drops the buffered text
# and goes out live. EVERY other kind - recorded entries and the
# engine's live-only kinds alike (steer, a job's notice, the "provider
# hiccup" system line) - takes a supervisor seq and reaches the page as a
# change signal (supervisor.go emitLocked).
#
# Page (web/src/app.tsx, streamAfter and the `selected` effect): a
# fragment extends the last run of its kind unless that run is sealed,
# else starts a run. A delta-reset clears the preview and zeroes
# `superseded`. Any other non-delta event sets superseded = runs (the
# runs on screen now belong to what was just recorded) and arms the
# 120ms catch-up. The catch-up captures drop = superseded when it starts,
# GETs ?since=<cursor>, and when the response has entries appends them
# and slices `drop` runs off the preview (superseded -= drop). A response
# with no entries changes neither. A failed catch-up re-arms.
#
# Fields: the engine's side (cur, disk, n, turn, steered, queued) and the page's
# (stream, sup, seen, timer, fetch, drop, header, cut). A fragment is an
# int, 1..MAXFRAG in arrival order, so "text" is a list of them; a run is
# record(kind, frags); an entry is record(kind, frags, partial). The
# page's lines are disk[:seen]: every catch-up appends a prefix of disk
# (the cursor is the last line's seq).
#
# Abstractions, each to keep the graph small:
# - one turn, MAXFRAG fragments over all of it, at most one steer. Two
#   fragments reach every race below: the clipped one needs a sealed run,
#   a reset and one fragment after it.
# - the 50ms buffer and the SSE stream are instant and lossless:
#   live_transcript_sync covers dropped subscribers and reconnects, and
#   the flush-before-record order makes the buffer invisible here.
# - Steer stands for every live-only change signal: it seals the preview
#   like a job notice or the hiccup line, and it is the one that also
#   records an entry at once, which is what makes its catch-up slice.
#   A live-only signal with nothing recorded returns early (no entries);
#   one with a record racing it is Steer's case with the entry later.
#   The steer comes while the first fragment's run streams, the page
#   otherwise quiet: both of its races (the slice of the unrecorded run,
#   and the reset after it) need exactly that, and a steer racing a
#   catch-up already in flight seals like the record racing it
#   (Respond), which the graph has. Anywhere else it multiplied the graph
#   by five (2159 states) and reached no claim the rest does not.
# - a response's records (thinking, assistant) and a close's (partial
#   assistant, cancelled, done) reach the page back to back with no
#   fragment between; each sets superseded to the same runs, so they are
#   one change signal here.
# - one catch-up in flight. In the product the timer can fire again while
#   one is pending, and both slice by their own drop.
# - the catch-up reads the transcript when it lands: the latest read it
#   can make, and the one that can see more than its drop was taken for.
#   A record between an earlier read and the landing seals the same runs
#   before the slice as after it (sup - drop either way), so landing-time
#   reads lose no page state.
#
# Fairness: the page's own steps (timer firing, the response landing) are
# strongly fair; so are the engine's Respond and Finish, the claim being
# about a turn that ends. Deltas, resets, the steer, Stop and a failed
# catch-up are not.
#
# Corrections to the mapped model, from the code:
# - "sealed_by_live_only_event" is not only steer/job/hiccup: `activity`,
#   call-delta and a native call's start return before the seal, every
#   other kind seals (a loop call's start too).
# - a response of only unsummarized thinking records nothing and sends no
#   change signal: its run stays on screen, unsealed, and the next
#   request's first thinking fragment EXTENDS it (one run across two
#   requests) until a later record's catch-up drops both. But only when a
#   queued steer is what asks the model again (actor.go response): every
#   other way a turn goes on past a response records something first. A
#   response the turn goes on from made a tool call, and the call's
#   recorded end (project.go call: a "call" entry) seals like any
#   record. So Respond appends a "call" entry unless a steer is `queued`
#   (found by the server walk, which cannot make the engine ask again
#   without one or the other).
# - a delta-reset clears sealed runs too (recorded, not yet fetched):
#   their text blinks out until that catch-up lands. Allowed by the
#   mapped claim ("except by a reset").
# - `left` (not in the mapped suspects; found here): a catch-up can read
#   entries recorded after its drop was captured. It appends them but
#   slices only `drop` runs, so the runs they sealed stay in the preview:
#   text shown twice, or unrecorded thinking left before lines it
#   precedes. The catch-up their own signal armed reads nothing new and
#   returns early without slicing, so they stay until a later entry
#   lands - after a turn's last entry, for good (the next turn's preview
#   starts with them).
#
# Where the product does not hold, a ghost says so and the claim it
# breaks is exempted by it (see each assertion): sliced, unsummarized,
# left, uncut. Remove an exemption and fizz fails.

MAXFRAG = 2

def seal(stream):
    # Any non-delta change signal: every run on screen is sealed.
    return len(stream)

def stream_after(stream, sealed, kind, f):
    # streamAfter: extend the last run of the same kind unless sealed.
    n = len(stream)
    if n > sealed and stream[n - 1].kind == kind:
        last = stream[n - 1]
        return stream[:n - 1] + [record(kind=kind, frags=last.frags + [f])]
    return stream + [record(kind=kind, frags=[f])]

def engine_after(cur, kind, f):
    # The engine's own view of the attempt: runs of one kind merge.
    n = len(cur)
    if n > 0 and cur[n - 1].kind == kind:
        last = cur[n - 1]
        return cur[:n - 1] + [record(kind=kind, frags=last.frags + [f])]
    return cur + [record(kind=kind, frags=[f])]

def frags_of(xs):
    out = []
    for x in xs:
        out = out + x.frags
    return out

def header_of(stream):
    # The Working label: "Thinking" iff the last run is thinking.
    if len(stream) == 0:
        return ""
    if stream[len(stream) - 1].kind == "thinking":
        return "Thinking"
    return "Working"

def lines_of(p):
    return frags_of(p.disk[:p.seen])

role Preview:
    action Init:
        # --- engine / server ---
        self.cur = []          # runs of the attempt in flight, unrecorded
        self.disk = []         # recorded entries: record(kind, frags, partial)
        self.n = 0             # fragments streamed so far
        self.turn = "running"  # running | done
        self.steered = False
        self.queued = False    # the steer's input waits for the request's boundary
        # --- page ---
        self.stream = []       # the preview: runs on screen
        self.sup = 0           # `superseded`: sealed runs
        self.seen = 0          # lines = disk[:seen]
        self.timer = False     # catch-up armed
        self.fetch = False     # a catch-up in flight
        self.drop = 0          # the runs it will slice, captured at its start
        self.header = ""       # "Thinking" | "Working" | "" (no preview)
        self.cut = False       # the page marks a cut reply (data.partial is never read)

    # --- Provider / engine ---

    atomic action ThinkingDelta:
        require self.turn == "running" and self.n < MAXFRAG
        self.n = self.n + 1
        self.cur = engine_after(self.cur, "thinking", self.n)
        self.stream = stream_after(self.stream, self.sup, "thinking", self.n)
        self.header = header_of(self.stream)

    atomic action TextDelta:
        require self.turn == "running" and self.n < MAXFRAG
        self.n = self.n + 1
        self.cur = engine_after(self.cur, "assistant", self.n)
        self.stream = stream_after(self.stream, self.sup, "assistant", self.n)
        self.header = header_of(self.stream)

    atomic fair<strong> action Respond:
        # System.RecordThinking/RecordAssistant: the response lands. A
        # reasoning item without a summary records nothing.
        require self.turn == "running" and len(self.cur) > 0
        thinks = len([r for r in self.cur if r.kind == "thinking"]) > 0
        opts = [True]
        if thinks:
            opts = [True, False]
        oneof summary in opts:
            recs = []
            for r in self.cur:
                if r.kind == "assistant" or summary:
                    recs.append(record(kind=r.kind, frags=r.frags, partial=False))
                else:
                    for f in r.frags:
                        unsummarized.append(f)
            # The turn goes on past the response through its tool call,
            # whose end is recorded, unless the queued steer carries it
            # on (see the corrections).
            if not self.queued:
                recs.append(record(kind="call", frags=[], partial=False))
            self.queued = False
            self.cur = []
            self.disk = self.disk + recs
            if len(recs) > 0:
                self.sup = seal(self.stream)
                self.timer = True

    atomic action DeltaReset:
        # A retry, or a newer request, after shown text.
        require self.turn == "running" and len(self.cur) > 0
        self.cur = []
        self.stream = []
        self.sup = 0
        self.header = ""

    atomic action Steer:
        # Live "steer", and the input entry recorded at once. Only while
        # the first run streams (see the abstractions).
        require self.turn == "running" and not self.steered and len(self.cur) > 0 and self.n == 1
        self.steered = True
        self.queued = True
        self.disk = self.disk + [record(kind="input", frags=[], partial=False)]
        self.sup = seal(self.stream)
        self.timer = True

    atomic action Stop:
        # User.Stop: the attempt's text is kept as a partial assistant
        # entry, its thinking is dropped; cancelled + done close the turn.
        # Only mid-stream: between requests it is Finish to the preview.
        require self.turn == "running" and len(self.cur) > 0
        text = []
        for r in self.cur:
            if r.kind == "assistant":
                text = text + r.frags
            else:
                for f in r.frags:
                    unsummarized.append(f)
        recs = []
        if len(text) > 0:
            recs = [record(kind="assistant", frags=text, partial=True)]
        self.cur = []
        self.queued = False
        self.disk = self.disk + recs + [record(kind="done", frags=[], partial=False)]
        self.turn = "done"
        self.sup = seal(self.stream)
        self.timer = True

    atomic fair<strong> action Finish:
        require self.turn == "running" and len(self.cur) == 0
        # A queued steer asks the model once more first; that answer
        # records nothing.
        self.queued = False
        self.disk = self.disk + [record(kind="done", frags=[], partial=False)]
        self.turn = "done"
        self.sup = seal(self.stream)
        self.timer = True

    # --- the page's catch-up ---

    atomic fair<strong> action CatchUpFire:
        require self.timer and not self.fetch
        self.timer = False
        self.fetch = True
        self.drop = self.sup

    atomic fair<strong> action CatchUpOk:
        require self.fetch
        d = self.drop
        self.fetch = False
        self.drop = 0
        if len(self.disk) > self.seen:
            self.seen = len(self.disk)
            lines = lines_of(self)
            gone = frags_of(self.stream[:d])
            self.stream = self.stream[d:]
            self.sup = max(0, self.sup - d)
            self.header = header_of(self.stream)
            for e in self.disk:
                if e.partial and not self.cut:
                    uncut = True
            # Ghosts: what this landing did that the claims below forbid.
            for g in gone:
                if g not in lines and g not in unsummarized and g not in sliced:
                    sliced.append(g)
            for g in frags_of(self.stream[:self.sup]):
                if g not in left:
                    left.append(g)

    atomic action CatchUpFails:
        require self.fetch
        self.fetch = False
        self.drop = 0
        self.timer = True

action Init:
    preview = Preview()
    # Ghosts (not product state, not compared by MBT). Product gaps, each the fragments (or the fact) a claim below exempts:
    unsummarized = []  # thinking no entry will ever hold (no summary, or cut by Stop)
    sliced = []        # unrecorded text a catch-up's stale drop sliced off
    left = []          # sealed runs a catch-up that read past its drop kept
    uncut = False      # a partial reply landed and renders complete

# No streamed fragment is shown twice (lines + preview).
# Except `left`: see the last correction above.
always assertion NoFragmentTwice:
    shown = lines_of(preview) + frags_of(preview.stream)
    seen = []
    for f in shown:
        if f in seen and f not in left:
            return False
        seen.append(f)
    return True

# Streamed text not yet recorded is never dropped, except by a reset or
# by its own record landing (text on disk is recorded: the claim is
# about the rest).
# Except `sliced`: suspect 1, a steer (or a job, a hiccup) seals the
# unrecorded reply and the catch-up its signal armed slices it off; and
# suspect 2, a reset during an in-flight catch-up zeroes superseded but
# the response still slices by the drop taken before it, so the retried
# reply's first run goes. Either comes back only when its record lands.
# And `unsummarized`: suspect 3, thinking with no summary, or cut by
# Stop, is never recorded, so its block leaves with the next record's
# runs and never comes back.
always assertion UnrecordedNeverDropped:
    here = lines_of(preview) + frags_of(preview.stream)
    recorded = frags_of(preview.disk)
    live = frags_of(preview.cur)
    for f in range(1, preview.n + 1):
        if f in here or f in recorded:
            continue
        # Discarded by a delta-reset: no longer in the attempt in flight
        # and not thinking left unrecorded (a reset is the one discard
        # that needs no ghost).
        if f not in live and f not in unsummarized:
            continue
        if f in sliced or f in unsummarized:
            continue
        return False
    return True

# After a response lands the preview holds only fragments after the
# recorded lines (never text the lines already show or passed).
# Except `left`: see the last correction above.
always assertion PreviewFollowsLines:
    lines = lines_of(preview)
    if len(lines) == 0:
        return True
    top = max(lines)
    for f in frags_of(preview.stream):
        if f <= top and f not in left:
            return False
    return True

# Thinking and text keep arrival order, in the lines and in the preview.
always assertion ArrivalOrder:
    for xs in [lines_of(preview), frags_of(preview.stream)]:
        for i in range(1, len(xs)):
            if xs[i] <= xs[i - 1]:
                return False
    return True

# The header says Thinking iff the last run is thinking.
always assertion HeaderMatchesLastRun:
    if len(preview.stream) == 0:
        return preview.header == ""
    last = preview.stream[len(preview.stream) - 1]
    return (preview.header == "Thinking") == (last.kind == "thinking")

# A partial (cancelled) reply is marked as cut.
# Except `uncut`: suspect 4, nothing on the page reads data.partial, and
# the cancelled turn is closed by its "done", so a cut answer renders
# complete.
always assertion PartialMarkedCut:
    if uncut:
        return True
    for e in preview.disk[:preview.seen]:
        if e.partial and not preview.cut:
            return False
    return True

# Every run is eventually replaced by its record or cleared.
# Except runs `left` behind (see the last correction above): nothing
# slices them once the turn's last entry has landed.
always eventually assertion RunsEventuallyReplaced:
    for f in frags_of(preview.stream):
        if f not in left:
            return False
    return True
